          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "The feeling you describe—a heavy squeezing behind your breastbone when you exert yourself (like walking uphill) that gets better when you rest—is a symptom that a clinician would want to discuss (red-flag-chest-pain).\n\nYour symptoms could involve your cardiovascular system or potentially your gastrointestinal system. Conditions a clinician might consider include angina, which is often associated with the heart, or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), which can cause chest discomfort (condition-gerd, red-flag-chest-pain).\n\nRegarding prevention strategies for angina, the diagnostic specialist states that the information they have does not specifically describe prevention strategies, and that this would be an important topic to discuss with a clinician if a diagnosis of angina is made (red-flag-chest-pain).\n\nClinical guidelines for the prevention and management of angina involve a combination of lifestyle modifications, medications, and sometimes procedures (condition-coronary-artery-disease). Management typically includes treating risk factors, antiplatelet and lipid-lowering therapies, medications to control angina symptoms, cardiac rehabilitation, and revascularization if symptoms persist or indicate it (Coronary artery disease, General clinical reference).\n\nLifestyle modifications are a key part of prevention and management, and these are recommended for everyone. They include:\n*   Reducing dietary sodium (Hypertension, General clinical reference).\n*   Eating a diet rich in vegetables, fruits, whole grains, and low-fat dairy, while being low in saturated fat (Hypertension, General clinical reference; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n*   Engaging in regular aerobic activity most days of the week (Hypertension, General clinical reference; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n*   Managing weight if it is above a healthy range (Hypertension, General clinical reference; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n*   Limiting alcohol intake (Hypertension, General clinical reference; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n*   Stopping smoking (Hypertension, General clinical reference; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\nThese changes can each reduce risk, and their effects are partly additive (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n\nPharmacological treatments may include antiplatelet therapy, lipid-lowering therapy (such as high-intensity statins), agents to control angina symptoms, and managing blood pressure to a target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people (Coronary artery disease, General clinical reference; Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation, Major cardiology society consensus guidance; Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis, Major cardiology society consensus guidance). Additionally, renin-angiotensin blockade may be used if diabetes, reduced left ventricular function, or chronic kidney disease is present, and beta-blockade might be used after a heart attack, especially with reduced left ventricular function (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n\nBeyond lifestyle changes, non-pharmacological interventions include cardiac rehabilitation, which is a structured program offering supervised exercise, education, and risk-factor management (Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n\nThere are some areas where guidance differs:\n*   United States guidance on lipid targets focuses on statin intensity and percentage reduction, while European guidance describes explicit LDL goals (Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n*   Different bodies have varying approaches to how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension, depending on the diagnostic blood pressure threshold used (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line, Major cardiology society consensus guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
